1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,628
"I cloned the service and the
vendor/textsplit directory is empty.

2
00:00:08,668 --> 00:00:09,764
The imports fail."

3
00:00:10,104 --> 00:00:14,111
"git pull worked for everyone yesterday.
Today it dies with not our ref."

4
00:00:14,451 --> 00:00:19,496
"I pulled, changed one line in ingest.py,
committed, and the reviewer says my commit

5
00:00:19,536 --> 00:00:20,949
downgrades the library."

6
00:00:21,289 --> 00:00:26,113
"CI is green on my branch and red on main,
and the only difference is a directory I

7
00:00:26,153 --> 00:00:26,992
never touched."

8
00:00:27,332 --> 00:00:32,573
All four are about a submodule: another
repository checked out in a subdirectory of

9
00:00:32,613 --> 00:00:37,919
yours. A CTO who hears these asks two things.
Why does this mechanism fail so often?

10
00:00:37,959 --> 00:00:40,019
And should we be using it at all?

11
00:00:40,359 --> 00:00:44,347
Both questions have precise answers,
and both start from one fact.

12
00:00:44,387 --> 00:00:49,351
The superproject, the repository that
contains the submodule, records a commit ID,

13
00:00:49,391 --> 00:00:54,294
not a branch, and Git's defaults don't
keep the nested checkout in sync with that

14
00:00:54,334 --> 00:00:54,721
record.

15
00:00:55,061 --> 00:00:59,472
Every standard submodule failure is a gap
between three things that should agree:

16
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:03,483
the commit ID recorded in the superproject,
the commit checked out in the

17
00:01:03,523 --> 00:01:07,769
submodule's working tree, and the commits
that exist in the submodule's shared

18
00:01:07,809 --> 00:01:08,374
repository.

19
00:01:10,214 --> 00:01:14,098
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

20
00:01:14,138 --> 00:01:19,437
This video opens the last module of this
part: three ways to bring something into a

21
00:01:19,477 --> 00:01:23,361
repository that doesn't live in
its history the ordinary way.

22
00:01:23,401 --> 00:01:25,548
Submodules, subtrees, and Git LFS.

23
00:01:25,888 --> 00:01:30,742
Today you answer the first report, the empty
directory, and you build the model that

24
00:01:30,782 --> 00:01:34,121
explains the other three. Hold
on to that empty directory.

25
00:01:34,161 --> 00:01:38,898
You need two things from earlier: the tree
object, Git's listing of one directory,

26
00:01:38,938 --> 00:01:41,869
with its entry modes, and
what git clone transfers.

27
00:01:42,209 --> 00:01:46,949
The projects are doc-qa, a document
question-answering service owned by your team,

28
00:01:46,989 --> 00:01:51,787
and textsplit, a small text-chunking library
that Asha maintains in a repository of

29
00:01:51,827 --> 00:01:54,177
its own. Ravi is your teammate on doc-qa.

30
00:01:56,017 --> 00:02:00,243
say what a superproject stores about
a submodule and where each piece is;

31
00:02:00,583 --> 00:02:05,577
read a gitlink entry in a tree and explain
why git cat-file cannot show its target;

32
00:02:05,917 --> 00:02:09,479
take a plain clone through init and
update and say what each writes;

33
00:02:09,819 --> 00:02:14,598
explain why a recursive clone of an
untrusted repository is a security decision.

34
00:02:16,438 --> 00:02:20,984
In one sentence: a submodule is another
repository checked out in a subdirectory of

35
00:02:21,024 --> 00:02:25,570
yours. Your repository records exactly one
thing about it in its history: the ID of

36
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:28,001
the commit that should
be checked out there.

37
00:02:28,341 --> 00:02:31,915
Precisely, there are three pieces,
in three different places.

38
00:02:32,255 --> 00:02:37,462
A fourth, local piece connects them.
git submodule init copies the URL from

39
00:02:37,502 --> 00:02:43,058
.gitmodules into the superproject's
.git/config. From then on Git uses the copy.

40
00:02:43,098 --> 00:02:47,605
That's why a changed URL doesn't
reach existing clones by itself.

41
00:02:47,945 --> 00:02:52,862
The point that the rest of the module depends
on: the superproject's object database

42
00:02:52,902 --> 00:02:57,643
doesn't contain the commit that the gitlink
names. A tree entry of mode 160000 is

43
00:02:57,683 --> 00:03:02,483
the only place in Git where an object ID
names something that the repository isn't

44
00:03:02,523 --> 00:03:07,322
required to have. Git doesn't check that
the ID exists anywhere when you commit it

45
00:03:07,362 --> 00:03:11,867
or when you push it. Everything that
makes the entry useful, where to get the

46
00:03:11,907 --> 00:03:16,706
object, whether it has been fetched, whether
it's checked out, is arranged outside

47
00:03:16,746 --> 00:03:17,356
the commit.

48
00:03:17,696 --> 00:03:22,496
What a teammate receives. A plain clone
transfers the superproject's objects.

49
00:03:22,536 --> 00:03:27,463
The gitlink arrives. The commit it names
doesn't. Two steps fill the directory.

50
00:03:27,803 --> 00:03:32,919
🟢 SAFE: git submodule init registers
the submodule by copying the URL into

51
00:03:32,959 --> 00:03:38,215
.git/config. 🟡 CAUTION: git submodule
update then clones the repository into

52
00:03:38,255 --> 00:03:43,928
.git/modules/ and checks out the recorded
commit. git submodule update --init does

53
00:03:43,968 --> 00:03:48,666
both, and --recursive repeats them
for submodules inside submodules.

54
00:03:48,706 --> 00:03:53,196
git clone --recurse-submodules
does everything for a fresh clone.

55
00:03:53,536 --> 00:03:59,267
The security side. Git has a per-transport
policy, protocol.<name>.allow, with three

56
00:03:59,307 --> 00:04:03,526
values: always, never and user.
The manual gives the defaults.

57
00:04:03,566 --> 00:04:08,748
http, https, git and ssh are always.
ext is never. Everything else, the file

58
00:04:08,788 --> 00:04:14,451
transport included, is user. Under user, a
transport may be used only when a person

59
00:04:14,491 --> 00:04:20,222
typed the clone, fetch or push. The git
submodule command marks its transfers as not

60
00:04:20,262 --> 00:04:25,512
typed by the user, because the URLs it
will use come from .gitmodules, a file

61
00:04:25,552 --> 00:04:28,466
written by whoever
authored the repository.

62
00:04:28,806 --> 00:04:35,082
The value user for file dates from the
fix for CVE-2022-39253, shipped in Git

63
00:04:35,122 --> 00:04:41,807
2.38.1. The lab's "servers" are bare
repositories in a directory, so the submodule

64
00:04:41,847 --> 00:04:48,368
URL is a file path, and each command that
makes Git clone a submodule on its own

65
00:04:48,408 --> 00:04:54,520
initiative is run with -c
protocol.file.allow=always, for that one command.

66
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:59,687
With a real remote over HTTPS or
SSH you never need the option.

67
00:05:00,027 --> 00:05:03,773
When not to use a recursive clone:
on a repository you don't trust.

68
00:05:03,813 --> 00:05:08,465
A recursive clone is the one common Git
operation in which a repository you haven't

69
00:05:08,505 --> 00:05:12,082
inspected decides what else gets
cloned and where it is written.

70
00:05:13,922 --> 00:05:19,203
The textbook's analogy: a recipe that says
"use the stock from page 212 of the other

71
00:05:19,243 --> 00:05:23,384
book, third printing". Your book
doesn't contain the stock recipe.

72
00:05:23,424 --> 00:05:26,742
It contains a precise reference
to one version of it.

73
00:05:26,782 --> 00:05:31,809
Anyone cooking from your book needs the
other book as well, opened at that page.

74
00:05:32,149 --> 00:05:36,462
Where the analogy breaks: the other book
keeps being reprinted, and your reference

75
00:05:36,502 --> 00:05:40,814
doesn't update itself. And the reader can
scribble in their copy of the other book

76
00:05:40,854 --> 00:05:43,308
without your book noticing,
until they compare.

77
00:05:43,648 --> 00:05:48,524
From this, three values that you'll compare
in every submodule diagnosis: the page

78
00:05:48,564 --> 00:05:53,500
your book cites, the page the reader's copy
is open at, and the pages that exist in

79
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:54,879
the bookshop's edition.

80
00:05:55,219 --> 00:05:59,890
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds.
Draw two boxes, doc-qa and textsplit.

81
00:05:59,930 --> 00:06:04,978
Then draw the two arrows that leave doc-qa,
and write on each what you predict it

82
00:06:05,018 --> 00:06:05,481
carries.

83
00:06:11,821 --> 00:06:15,574
Check your drawing. Two arrows
leave the superproject's box.

84
00:06:15,614 --> 00:06:20,885
One carries a URL, from a versioned file.
The other carries a commit ID, from a tree

85
00:06:20,925 --> 00:06:26,196
entry. Neither carries any content of the
library. The three lines at the bottom are

86
00:06:26,236 --> 00:06:28,598
local: a plain clone has none of them.

87
00:06:30,438 --> 00:06:35,173
"transport 'file' not allowed". That's the
transport policy, and you'll return to

88
00:06:35,213 --> 00:06:39,417
it. For the lab's directory remotes,
the option goes on the one command.

89
00:06:39,757 --> 00:06:44,568
The command cloned the library into
vendor/textsplit and staged two things.

90
00:06:44,608 --> 00:06:44,957
First:

91
00:06:45,297 --> 00:06:49,505
The versioned file holds the
relative URL ../textsplit.git.

92
00:06:49,545 --> 00:06:55,338
A URL that starts with one or two dots is
resolved against the superproject's own

93
00:06:55,378 --> 00:07:00,235
remote. The local configuration
holds the resolved, absolute result.

94
00:07:00,275 --> 00:07:06,211
Relative URLs are worth using when both
repositories live on the same host: a clone

95
00:07:06,251 --> 00:07:12,044
made over HTTPS fetches the submodule over
HTTPS, a clone made over SSH uses SSH,

96
00:07:12,084 --> 00:07:15,573
and no protocol is written
into a versioned file.

97
00:07:15,913 --> 00:07:20,564
Second, the gitlink. Predict the mode
of the entry for vendor/textsplit.

98
00:07:20,604 --> 00:07:22,324
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

99
00:07:27,164 --> 00:07:32,242
Four blobs with mode 100644 and
one entry with mode 160000.

100
00:07:32,282 --> 00:07:34,758
That last one is the gitlink.

101
00:07:35,098 --> 00:07:40,327
This is the library's own history. The ID
in the entry, e216665, is the commit that

102
00:07:40,367 --> 00:07:43,819
the library's main pointed
at when you ran the command.

103
00:07:43,859 --> 00:07:45,914
Nothing in the entry says "main".

104
00:07:46,254 --> 00:07:51,067
The submodule's working tree has a .git
file, the same device a linked worktree

105
00:07:51,107 --> 00:07:51,374
uses.

106
00:07:51,714 --> 00:07:55,665
The real Git directory sits under
the superproject's .git/modules/.

107
00:07:55,705 --> 00:08:00,312
Keeping it there means the repository
survives when the working tree directory

108
00:08:00,352 --> 00:08:05,257
disappears, for example when you switch to
a commit of the superproject from before

109
00:08:05,297 --> 00:08:06,568
the submodule existed.

110
00:08:06,908 --> 00:08:11,432
To the superproject a submodule is a
one-line file whose content is "Subproject

111
00:08:11,472 --> 00:08:15,939
commit" and an ID. That's how every later
change to it appears in diffs and in

112
00:08:15,979 --> 00:08:16,343
review.

113
00:08:16,683 --> 00:08:19,425
git ls-tree prints the
type commit for the entry.

114
00:08:19,765 --> 00:08:25,138
The tree entry names commit e216665.
Predict: can the superproject show you that

115
00:08:25,178 --> 00:08:26,356
object? I'll wait.

116
00:08:31,196 --> 00:08:32,648
"could not get object info".

117
00:08:32,988 --> 00:08:37,525
The commit object isn't in this repository.
It lives in the object database of the

118
00:08:37,565 --> 00:08:38,083
submodule.

119
00:08:43,423 --> 00:08:46,999
Read the three together. The
directory exists and is empty.

120
00:08:47,039 --> 00:08:52,086
git status says "nothing to commit, working
tree clean": an uninitialized submodule

121
00:08:52,126 --> 00:08:57,050
isn't a modification, so nothing warns
Ravi. Only git submodule status tells him,

122
00:08:57,090 --> 00:09:01,769
with the leading minus sign. That's
report 1, the empty directory, explained.

123
00:09:02,109 --> 00:09:06,807
init copied the URL into
.git/config. Nothing was downloaded.

124
00:09:07,147 --> 00:09:08,512
"checked out" and the full ID.

125
00:09:08,852 --> 00:09:12,953
The directory is populated, and the
status line now starts with a space.

126
00:09:13,293 --> 00:09:18,552
One step we skip today. The lab's snippet
named 04-detached shows that HEAD in the

127
00:09:18,592 --> 00:09:23,529
submodule is detached after update.
That's the first topic of the next video.

128
00:09:23,869 --> 00:09:28,843
Now the recursive clone, without the option.
A quick quiz: after this command, is

129
00:09:28,883 --> 00:09:32,063
the superproject there, or
is nothing cloned at all?

130
00:09:32,103 --> 00:09:33,053
Say it out loud.

131
00:09:37,893 --> 00:09:42,801
The superproject is there. Its clone
worked. Only the clone that Git started by

132
00:09:42,841 --> 00:09:43,991
itself was refused.

133
00:09:44,331 --> 00:09:46,656
The second command
reproduces the mechanism.

134
00:09:46,996 --> 00:09:52,238
With the variable set to 0, Git treats even
a clone you typed as one the user didn't

135
00:09:52,278 --> 00:09:54,753
type, and the file transport is refused.

136
00:09:55,093 --> 00:09:58,331
With the option on the one command,
the recursive clone completes:

137
00:10:08,871 --> 00:10:14,017
Both are fixed in the Git 2.55.0 this
course uses. The second advisory's own

138
00:10:14,057 --> 00:10:19,681
workaround is the durable rule: do not
recursively clone submodules of repositories

139
00:10:19,721 --> 00:10:24,935
you do not trust. Clone without recursion,
read .gitmodules, and then decide.

140
00:10:26,775 --> 00:10:28,423
Five mistakes to watch for.

141
00:10:28,763 --> 00:10:31,991
"The clone is broken, the
vendor directory is empty."

142
00:10:32,031 --> 00:10:36,924
Root cause: a plain clone transfers the
gitlink and not the commit it names; the

143
00:10:36,964 --> 00:10:39,637
submodule has to be
initialized and updated.

144
00:10:39,977 --> 00:10:44,959
Trusting git status to report it. Root
cause: an uninitialized submodule is not a

145
00:10:44,999 --> 00:10:48,927
modification; only git submodule
status shows the leading minus.

146
00:10:49,267 --> 00:10:54,117
Believing the submodule follows a branch.
Root cause: the gitlink is a commit ID;

147
00:10:54,157 --> 00:10:56,290
nothing in the entry names a branch.

148
00:10:56,630 --> 00:11:01,972
Setting protocol.file.allow=always
globally. Root cause: it removes, for every

149
00:11:02,012 --> 00:11:07,423
repository, the default that stops Git
from using the file transport on its own

150
00:11:07,463 --> 00:11:08,182
initiative.

151
00:11:08,522 --> 00:11:11,974
git clone --recurse-submodules
on an unknown repository.

152
00:11:12,014 --> 00:11:17,150
Root cause: the repository's .gitmodules
then decides what else is cloned and where

153
00:11:17,190 --> 00:11:21,514
it is written, which is where recent
client vulnerabilities have been.

154
00:11:23,354 --> 00:11:28,229
Now, out of the lab. A team keeps prompt
templates, a tokenizer vocabulary and

155
00:11:28,269 --> 00:11:32,388
protocol definitions in one repository
that five services consume.

156
00:11:32,428 --> 00:11:35,916
A submodule gives each service
an exact, reviewable pin.

157
00:11:35,956 --> 00:11:41,146
The pull request that moves the pin shows
one line removed and one line added, each

158
00:11:41,186 --> 00:11:46,249
"Subproject commit" with an ID. A release
of the service is reproducible from one

159
00:11:46,289 --> 00:11:48,140
commit ID of the superproject.

160
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:51,935
The price is the set of
failures on the first slide.

161
00:11:51,975 --> 00:11:56,236
So the team's onboarding page has
three lines for new engineers.

162
00:11:56,276 --> 00:12:01,747
Clone with --recurse-submodules. If a vendor
directory is empty, run git submodule

163
00:12:01,787 --> 00:12:07,124
update --init. And read git submodule
status before you ask why an import fails.

164
00:12:08,964 --> 00:12:16,144
Your turn. Do Exercise 15.1, Level 1, "Add
a submodule and read what was recorded",

165
00:12:16,184 --> 00:12:20,233
in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

166
00:12:20,573 --> 00:12:24,915
Before you look, write down the three places
where something was recorded and what

167
00:12:24,955 --> 00:12:29,136
each holds. Predict the mode and type
that git ls-tree prints for the submodule

168
00:12:29,176 --> 00:12:33,465
path, and predict the result of asking the
superproject for the object behind it.

169
00:12:33,805 --> 00:12:39,332
The challenge is Exercise 15.7, Level 3,
"An empty directory on the build machine",

170
00:12:39,372 --> 00:12:40,472
in the same file.

171
00:12:44,312 --> 00:12:49,289
Answer out loud. A strong answer separates
what is versioned from what is local and

172
00:12:49,329 --> 00:12:54,185
gives a location for each piece: a tree
entry with its mode, a file at the top of

173
00:12:54,225 --> 00:12:58,960
the working tree, a directory under the
Git directory, and a configuration key.

174
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:03,856
It says in so many words what the
superproject's object database doesn't contain,

175
00:13:03,896 --> 00:13:07,543
and what Git doesn't check when
you commit or push the entry.

176
00:13:07,583 --> 00:13:12,621
And it draws one consequence for a teammate's
fresh clone and one for a changed URL.

177
00:13:14,461 --> 00:13:19,755
Let's land this. One commit in the library's
own history, e216665: that's all your

178
00:13:19,795 --> 00:13:25,154
repository records about it. You explained
the first report, and you have the model

179
00:13:25,194 --> 00:13:26,454
for the other three.

180
00:13:26,794 --> 00:13:45,511
You should now be able to say:

181
00:13:45,851 --> 00:13:51,856
A submodule is a gitlink, a tree entry of
mode 160000 that holds a commit ID; a URL

182
00:13:51,896 --> 00:13:56,299
in .gitmodules; and a separate
repository under .git/modules.

183
00:13:56,639 --> 00:14:01,123
The superproject does not contain the
submodule's objects and does not check that

184
00:14:01,163 --> 00:14:02,631
the recorded commit exists.

185
00:14:02,971 --> 00:14:07,802
A plain clone leaves the directory empty
and git status clean; init copies the URL

186
00:14:07,842 --> 00:14:10,950
and update clones and checks
out the recorded commit.

187
00:14:11,290 --> 00:14:16,497
The first character of git submodule status
is the diagnosis: space, minus, plus or

188
00:14:16,537 --> 00:14:16,623
U.

189
00:14:16,963 --> 00:14:22,039
A recursive clone lets the cloned repository
decide what else is fetched, so for

190
00:14:22,079 --> 00:14:25,555
untrusted repositories I clone
without recursion first.

191
00:14:27,395 --> 00:14:32,073
